/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  ratebased04_schulz_delft_0p02bar  --  the SAME packed bed as packing04_schulz_delft_0p02bar, solved
  as RATE-BASED (non-equilibrium) segments: `model rateBased;` (DEV.md 4c
  C329 part 2, slice B1).

  WHAT THIS CASE IS.  Schulz et al.'s FRI bed (Montz-Pak A3-500M, 1.22 m,
  2.2 m of packing, p-/o-xylene at total reflux, 0.02 bar at the top; Chem.
  Eng. Trans. 69 (2018) 763) with every input of packing04_schulz_delft_0p02bar
  unchanged: geometry, top pressure, reboiler liquid, F-factor sweep, liquid
  viscosity.  What changes is the column model.  The transfer-unit bed
  integrates dy/dz = (y* - y)/H_OG with the vapour and the liquid
  exchanging equal and opposite moles at one temperature; here each of 100
  segments carries the bulk vapour and liquid, the interface compositions,
  the two fluxes and three temperatures (vapour, liquid, interface), and
  the two fluxes are set by the two films AND the interface energy balance.
  Equations, count, derivation: Theory Guide, section "The rate-based packed
  segment" (sec:rateBasedSegment); code RateBasedSegment.{H,cpp}.

  THE CORRELATION: the Delft model (Olujic, Kamerbeek & de Graauw 1999,
  in the working form of Fair et al., IECR 39 (2000) 1788, Tables 2 and 4) -- the film coefficients
  k_G, k_L and the effective area a_e, as in packing04_schulz_delft_0p02bar.

  WHAT IT SHOWS THAT THE TRANSFER-UNIT BED CANNOT: the interface temperature
  between the two bulk temperatures along the bed (the rising vapour is
  superheated over the interface by a few tenths of a kelvin), the ratio of
  the two fluxes (not -1: the two xylenes' latent heats differ), the vapour
  flow changing up the bed, and the liquid film's share of the resistance.
  On the HETP the difference from packing04_schulz_delft_0p02bar is a few tenths
  of a per cent at most: two isomers with nearly equal latent heats are the
  system where the two models SHOULD agree.  That is a measurement of the
  model, not a claim that it is closer to the experiment.

  AGAINST THE MEASUREMENT: the comparison with Schulz Fig. 2b (digitised in
  docs/design/c329-ratebased-first-delivery.md, sections 11-12) is tabulated
  in the Theory Guide beside the transfer-unit bed's; the rate model is
  within a fraction of a per cent of the transfer-unit bed at every point,
  so it carries the same errors against the measurement.

  INPUTS: as packing04_schulz_delft_0p02bar (its header lists which are measured,
    estimated and assumed; the corrugation geometry is ESTIMATED there).
  ADDED for the film heat-transfer coefficients: the thermal conductivity of
    both phases (constant/thermoPhysPropDict), estimated by Eucken (vapour)
    and Sato-Riedel (liquid); the coefficients follow from the packing
    model's own k_G and k_L by the heat/mass-transfer analogy.
  FITTED: nothing.
  WHAT IT DOES NOT CLAIM: that the rate model predicts the measured HETP
    better (it does not: see the table); that the heat-transfer analogy was
    checked against a measurement (none in the tree); anything about axial
    mixing or maldistribution (plug flow in both phases, stated in the run).

  THE SWEEP moves the boil-up F-factor exactly as packing04_schulz_delft_0p02bar does.
  The KPI rows of `expected` are those of the sweep point the driver
  publishes, the converged point nearest the middle of the range: fFactor
  1.8 (the declared 1.5 is what the case runs without its outerDict).
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        column;
        type        distillationColumn;
        model       rateBased;

        operation
        {
            P              0.02 bar;      // TOP pressure
            condenser      { type total; }

            totalReflux
            {
                fFactor        1.5;      // swept; (m/s)(kg/m3)^0.5, reboiler vapour on the tower area
                specifiedAt    reboiler;
                composition
                {
                    pXylene    0.17;
                    oXylene    0.83;
                }
            }

            packing
            {
                model             Delft;
                diameter          1.22 m;      // Schulz section 2
                height            2.2 m;       // Schulz section 2
                specificArea      478;         // m2/m3, installed (Schulz section 2)
                voidFraction      0.92;        // Schulz section 2
                corrugationAngle  60;          // deg (Schulz section 2)
                corrugationBase   0.01152 m;   // ESTIMATED: Montz B1-400 shape scaled to a_p = 478
                corrugationHeight 0.00609 m;   // ESTIMATED: as above
                elementHeight     0.169 m;     // 2.2 m / 13 layers (Schulz section 2)
                holeFraction      0;           // unperforated (Schulz section 2)
                lightKey          pXylene;
                liquidViscosity   5.39e-4;       // Pa s, Schulz Table 1
                segments          100;
            }
        }
    }
);
